# JavaScript Tutorial Section Rule

When the user says **"apply to \<Section\>"** (or similar, e.g. `@javascript_tutorial.mdc apply to JS Introduction`), document that tutorial section from the **open browser tab** (or the site they name, default [W3Schools JavaScript](https://www.w3schools.com/js/default.asp)).

They may also name a **range**: **"apply to \<Start\> to \<End\>"** (e.g. `apply to JS Introduction to JS Comments`). Process every left/sidebar item from Start through End **inclusive**, in nav order. One named section = one accordion. A range = one accordion per section in that span. Do **not** crawl the rest of the course, skip items in the range, or continue past End. If Start or End does not match a nav item, list nearby items and stop — do not guess. Finish each section (read → sandbox → run → snap → append) before starting the next.

This output is a **detailed study tutorial**, not a thin recap. The reader must be able to learn the whole section from the accordion alone — without ever opening W3Schools. That means **every idea explained in depth, every site example rebuilt and run, extra clarifying examples added where they help, and every result shown as a snap with a written description of what happened.** When in doubt, add more explanation and more examples, not less.

## Workflow Steps

1. **Read** `Personal/bin/format.md` once for **accordion spacing and Q&A nesting only**. Ignore its Lab and Code children — they are removed in this rule. This rule’s heading order and Detailed Explanation layout win.
2. **Open the section in the browser**
   - Default site: [https://www.w3schools.com/js/default.asp](https://www.w3schools.com/js/default.asp).
   - Use the existing Cursor browser tab if it is already on that tutorial.
   - If not, navigate to the site, then click the named item in the left/sidebar (or in-page heading). Example: **JS Introduction**.
   - If the name does not match, list nearby nav items and stop — do not guess a different section.
   - Scroll the **full** section. Read every heading, note, example, exercise, table, and warning. Capture the **section URL**.
3. **Rebuild examples in the sandbox**
   - Create `Personal/Files/javascript/code_sandbox/<slug>/` (`JS Introduction` → `js-introduction`).
   - Write runnable files. Prefer `index.html` with the section’s scripts (inline `<script>` or `script.js` if the page uses a separate file). Extra `.css` only if the section uses it. Extra HTML files (`source.html`, extra demos) when the page has several Tryit examples.
   - Recreate **every** Tryit / runnable example on the page. Do not invent unrelated features, and do not drop examples to save space. You **may** add small extra variations when they make a concept clearer — label them as your own example.
   - If the section is **console-only** (no DOM output), still keep a small `index.html` when the tutorial shows a browser Tryit, **or** use a `.js` file run with `node` when the page is clearly Node/console. Match the tutorial’s environment.
   - If the section has **no** runnable example, skip the run/snap steps and say so in Detailed Explanation.
4. **Run the example**
   - Serve browser examples over **http** (Cursor browser blocks `file://`). From `code_sandbox`, use a local static server, then open `http://127.0.0.1:<port>/<slug>/`.
   - Confirm the page matches the tutorial example (heading, output, button/alert behavior).
   - For console-only Node examples, run `node <file>.js` and treat that stdout as the result.
   - If the example **errors** or will not run, research a fix — prefer **official docs** (MDN JavaScript, ECMA-262). Tutorial sites often keep older snippets after the language or browsers have moved on. Use the current correct syntax in the sandbox, and note the mismatch in Detailed Explanation (what the page showed vs what works now). Do not invent unrelated features.
   - Stop the server when snaps are done.
5. **Snap source and result**
   - **Result:** screenshot the **running** sandbox page in the Cursor browser (or the terminal output for Node-only examples). That is the outcome the reader must see.
   - **Code:** snap the sandbox source. Prefer **CodeSnap** (`CodeSnap 📸` / `codesnap.start`) on the editor selection. If the agent cannot fire CodeSnap, screenshot the example as shown (tutorial editor or sandbox source) and still save a PNG.
   - Save PNGs under `Personal/Files/javascript/code_sandbox/snaps/`. Use **per-example numbering** so each example has its own pair:
     - `<slug>-01-code.png`, `<slug>-02-code.png`, …
     - `<slug>-01-result.png`, `<slug>-02-result.png`, …
     - A single-example section may use `<slug>-code.png` / `<slug>-result.png`.
   - Embed **relative** images from `tutorial.md`: `./code_sandbox/snaps/<file>.png`
6. **Append** one formatted accordion per section to `Personal/Files/javascript/tutorial.md`. **Never overwrite** the file. If an accordion with the same `<summary>` already exists, ask before replacing that block.
7. If `tutorial.md` has no `#` title yet, start the file with `# JavaScript Tutorial` then the accordion.

## Format Requirements

Accordion **spacing** matches `Personal/.cursor/rules/notes_doc.mdc` and `Personal/bin/format.md`. Heading **order and children** follow this rule:

- Topic accordion `<summary>` = the section title the user named (e.g. `JS Introduction`).
- **Accordion spacing:** one blank line after every `</summary>` and one blank line before every `</details>`.
- **Introduction**, **Detailed Explanation**, **Summary**, **References** = plain `##` headings (not child accordions).
- After Detailed Explanation, only these **closed child accordions**, in this order:
  1. **Terminal Commands**
  2. **Questions and Answers**
- **No `Lab` section.** **No separate `Code` section.** All prose, fenced code, code snaps, and result snaps live **inside Detailed Explanation**, next to the idea they demonstrate.
- Q&A: **8–15** questions that cover the actual examples (inputs, outputs, pitfalls), not just definitions; each answer is nested `<details><summary>Answer</summary>` with **only** `- [x]` lines.
- First References link = the **section URL** from the browser. Then other links from that page (and MDN / ECMA-262 when you used them).
- If a snap file is missing, do not fake an `<img>` path. Finish the snaps first.

### Detailed Explanation (the merged study body)

This is the heart of the tutorial and absorbs what used to be the separate **Code** section. Write it as a **guided, example-driven walkthrough**. Start with a short `- [x]` concept list, then walk **every** example from the page in order (plus any extra variations you add). **For each example, always include all five parts, in this order:**

1. **Heading** — `### **Example N: Title**` (title from the page, or a clear label).
2. **Explanation** — `- [x]` lines describing **what it does, why it works, and what to notice** (scope, quotes, return values, edge cases, pitfalls, "do not" advice). Be generous: multiple bullets and nested sub-points, not one sentence. Explain any table, note, or warning here in the same style.
3. **Tested code** — a fenced block (`javascript` / `html` / `css`) with the **actual sandbox source that ran** (never an untested paste from the site). Mention the sandbox path, e.g. `Sandbox: code_sandbox/<slug>/index.html`.
4. **Code snap** — `<img ... src="./code_sandbox/snaps/<slug>-NN-code.png" />` right after the fence.
5. **Result snap + written outcome** — `<img ... src="./code_sandbox/snaps/<slug>-NN-result.png" />`, immediately followed by at least one `- [x]` line that **describes the outcome in words** ("the paragraph now reads **Hello JavaScript**", "the console logs `15`"). The image must never be the only explanation of the result.

Cover **all** examples this way — do not summarize several into one. If official docs required newer syntax than the page, keep **both**: the page’s idea and the working current form, and say how they differ. Prefer thoroughness: more examples and richer explanation is the goal.

